# Lifecycle workflow rule

This repo follows the gated lifecycle in `docs/framework/00-overview.md`:
intake (G0) → PRD approved (G1) → plan approved (G2) → code complete (G3) →
tests green (G4) → ready to deploy (G5).

## Before acting, locate the feature

- Identify the `REQ-<id>` from the branch name, PR title, open files, or the
  user's message. Its artifacts are in `docs/features/REQ-<id>-<slug>/`.
- Read `prd.md` status and `technical-plan.md` status before generating code.
  If the plan is not `Approved`, do not write production code; explain the
  unmet gate and offer `/tech-plan` or `/clarify`.

## Assumption discipline

- Never resolve an ambiguity by silently choosing. Write
  `[ASSUMED: …]` at the point of use and add an `OQ-n` entry to
  `open-questions.md` (type Assumption, severity, options, recommendation,
  owner).
- Do not write a test whose expected value depends on an assumption that is
  not `Accepted` in the register.
- Prefer asking one batched set of questions over many single questions.

## IDs and traceability

- Reference `REQ-`, `FR-`, `AC-`, `NFR-`, `OQ-` IDs in plans, comments, test
  names, commit messages and PR text.
- When implementing, name the plan step you are executing and tick it in
  `technical-plan.md` §6 when done.

## Commits

- One logical change per commit. Format:
  `REQ-<id>: <imperative summary>` (≤ 72 chars), body lists FR/AC/OQ touched.
- Never amend or force-push unless explicitly told to.

## Templates

- New artifacts always start from `docs/templates/`. Keep section headings
  and IDs intact so tooling and reviewers can find them.
- Approved documents contain no `TBD`; use `OQ-n` references instead.

## Output style

- Explain *why* before showing code when the reason is not obvious from the
  plan.
- Keep diffs to the requested step. Mention, but do not make, unrelated
  improvements you notice.
